# Base-image CVEs that the pinned Alpine branch has not shipped a fix for yet.
#
# EMPTY, ON PURPOSE, and the file stays because both Trivy gates `cat` it --
# .forgejo/workflows/ci.yml and .github/workflows/release.yml. Deleting it
# would fail those steps rather than relax them.
#
# What was here, and why it went (2026-09-11):
#
#   Seven util-linux advisories -- CVE-2026-53612, -53613, -53614, -76642,
#   -78408, -78409, -78410 -- reported against libblkid, libmount and libuuid
#   2.42.1-r0 in the python:3.14.7-alpine base, 21 HIGH findings in all. Added
#   2026-09-05 because Trivy named a fix (2.42.3-r0) that existed only in
#   Alpine edge, so the Dockerfile's `apk upgrade` could not reach it, and main
#   went red four runs in a row.
#
#   That entry carried its own expiry: "remove these lines the day
#   `apk policy libblkid` in the base shows 2.42.3-r0". Alpine v3.24/main now
#   offers 2.42.3-r1, and a cache-free build of the `pybase` stage installs it,
#   so `apk upgrade` reaches the fix unaided and the suppressions describe a
#   state the image is no longer in. Removed rather than left standing: an
#   exception nobody revisits is how a real finding gets waved through.
#
# ONE CAVEAT worth knowing before this file is next touched. `apk upgrade` is a
# RUN layer keyed on this Dockerfile's text, not on time, so a build that
# reuses a cached `pybase` from before 2026-09-11 still carries 2.42.1-r0 and
# will now fail the gate instead of being waved past it. That failure is the
# correct one -- it says the image really does contain those CVEs. Bust the
# cache (`docker build --no-cache-filter pybase`) rather than restoring these
# lines.
#
# Adding an entry here: give the CVE id, the package and version, the date, why
# the fix cannot be reached today, and the condition under which the line comes
# out again. A bare id with no expiry is a permanent silent exception.
